sl2EquivariantConstantRankMatrix -- computes a SL(2)-equivariant constant rank matrix
*************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        M = sl2EquivariantConstantRankMatrix(d,m)
        M = sl2EquivariantConstantRankMatrix(d,m,CoefficientRing=>C)
        M = sl2EquivariantConstantRankMatrix(R,m)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, with at
        least two variables
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, positive
      * m, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, at least 2
      * C, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note CoefficientRing:
        sl2EquivariantConstantRankMatrix_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_
        pd_rp, => ..., default value QQ, name for optional argument
  * Outputs:
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, 

Description
===========

This function returns a constant rank matrix of linear forms. The matrix
describes the morphism

$\Phi: S^{md-2}V \otimes O_{\PP^d} \to S^{(m-1)d}V \otimes O_{\PP^d)}(1)$

given by the projection

$S^dV \otimes S^{(m-1)d}V \to S^{md-2}V$

of the irreducible $SL(2)$-subrepresentation of highest weight $md-2$, where
$\PP^d = \PP(S^dV)$ as $V=<v_0,v_1>$. In the paper A construction of
equivariant bundles on the space of symmetric forms, the entries of the matrix
$\Phi$ are explicitly described.

